<BlogPost slug="does-ozempic-help-with-alcohol-cravings" toc={toc}>
  <p>Ozempic is an FDA-approved medication for type 2 diabetes, but it has also recently gained popularity as an off-label drug for weight loss. In this context, it reduces appetite and leads to a calorie-deficient diet that contributes to weight loss&mdash;and the majority of people who take Ozempic do so for this purpose.</p>
  <p>Alcohol is a high-calorie drink, and people considering Ozempic often wonder whether it specifically lowers alcohol cravings. The simple answer is yes, Ozempic can help, but it is not a medication designed to treat <a href="/blog/what-is-dual-diagnosis/">alcohol use disorder</a> (AUD), and it should not substitute structured recovery care.</p>
  <p>In this article, I explain what Ozempic is, how it works, and why it isn&rsquo;t a stand-alone treatment for AUD.</p>

  <h2 id="what-is">What Is Ozempic?</h2>
  <p>Ozempic is the brand name of a medication containing semaglutide. It is prescribed to adults who have type 2 diabetes to help improve blood sugar levels. It can also lower the risk of stroke, heart attack, and heart disease, as well as kidney disease, among people with type 2 diabetes.</p>
  <p>As an off-label prescription, Ozempic is used for weight management and loss. It&rsquo;s less commonly used to treat Alzheimer&rsquo;s disease and polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS).</p>
  <p>Ozempic is available in pill form or as an injection. Pills are taken once per day, usually in the morning before eating food. Injections are self-administered once a week, via an Ozempic pen.</p>

